Please see the implementation below:

```cs
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

public static void Main(string[] args)
{
    var str = @"
Using System;
Using System.Text;
Using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
Using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Settings;
Using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Exceptions;
Using NUnit.Framework;
Using ArrayList;
Using PexAPIWrapper;

Namespace ArrayList.Test
{...}";

    // Define regex pattern
    var reg = new Regex(@"//\s*@@@(.*)$", RegexOptions.Multiline);
    // Find matches
    IList<string> matches = reg.Matches(str);

    if (matches.Count > 0)
    {
        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            // Replace carriage returns and double spaces with single spaces to avoid any extra spaces
            var comment = match.Groups[1].Value.Replace("  ", " ").Replace(Environment.NewLine, " ");
            
            // Remove leading or trailing spaces from the comment
            comment = comment.Trim();

            // Output
            Console.WriteLine("<code>");
            Console.WriteLine(@$"Debug.Assert({comment});");
            Console.WriteLine("<\code>");
        }
    }
}
```
The code first defines a regular expression pattern to match comment lines that start with "@@@". It then uses this pattern to match the desired comments within the given string, which should be csharp code. The matches are then looped through and the comments are extracted, while the carriage return and double spaces are removed. Finally, the formatted assert statement string is presented to the console.

This is a basic example and doesn't handle cases where the assertions contain complex statements or require additional formatting. For actual usage, additional considerations should be made regarding these. It's possible you need to refine the above code to fit your specific requirements.--------------------Before proceeding, please install "Pyright" which is a Static Type Checker (for both Python and TypeScript) by Microsoft that provides the ability to analyze JavaScript, TypeScript, and even Python, with rich error messages.--------------------